<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Crisis</p><h1 class="enc-title">Third Taiwan Strait Crisis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1995">1995</a>–<a href="/years/1996">1996</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Third <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan-strait">Taiwan Strait</a> Crisis was a period of military tension in 1995–1996, triggered by Taiwanese President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lee-teng-hui">Lee Teng-hui</a>'s visit to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in June 1995. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a> fired missiles into waters near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> and held exercises before Taiwan's first direct presidential election in March 1996. The United States sent two aircraft carrier groups to the region, and Lee won the election.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In 1994, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lee-teng-hui">Lee Teng-hui</a>'s plane stopped to refuel in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honolulu">Honolulu</a>, the administration of President Bill Clinton refused him a visa and he spent the night on his plane, a slight he complained of as treatment for a second-class leader. When he was invited to speak on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a>'s democratization at Cornell University, his alma mater, Secretary of State Warren Christopher assured the Chinese foreign minister Qian Qichen that a visa would be inconsistent with the unofficial relationship with Taiwan.</p><p>Congress, lobbied by the firm Cassidy &amp; Associates, passed a resolution in May 1995 by 396 votes to 0 in the House and 97 to 1 in the Senate, and the State Department gave way on 22 May. At Cornell on 9–10 June Lee called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> a country with independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>, which the PRC leadership called an effort to split the motherland.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>From 21 to 28 July 1995 the Second Artillery Corps fired missiles into areas north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a>, and naval and amphibious exercises followed through the autumn, while the People's Republic told Washington and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taipei">Taipei</a> it did not intend to invade. American warships passed through the strait, the USS Nimitz and its battle group on 19 December 1995. In early 1996 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> massed 100,000 troops along the strait before Taiwan's first presidential election.</p><p>On 8 March it fired missiles into waters off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keelung">Keelung</a> and Kaohsiung, ports that handled over 70 per cent of commercial shipping, and announced live-fire exercises near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penghu">Penghu</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> sent the USS Independence and then the Nimitz group, and from 18 to 25 March 150,000 troops rehearsed landings at Pingtan Island.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Shipping and insurance costs for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> rose sharply, and many in Taiwan feared the seizure of small outlying islands such as Wuqiu. The crisis lifted Lee by 5 per cent in the polls, giving him a majority on 23 March 1996.</p><p>PLA commanders such as Zhang Wannian called the exercises a success, but the army judged it lacked leverage against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, and Jiang ordered a ten-year modernization that brought Sovremenny-class destroyers, Kilo class submarines and Su-30 warplanes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. The crisis also strengthened the case for U.S. arms sales to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> and tightened military ties between the United States and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. In 1999 Major General Liu Liankun and Senior Colonel Shao Zhengzhong were executed for revealing to Taiwan that the missiles carried unarmed warheads.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Third Taiwan Strait Crisis” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Missile tests near Taiwan in 1995–96 provoked the <b>Third Taiwan Strait Crisis</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China · Military</a></li></ul></section>
